## Overview
Create a minimal test code change that can be applied to verify that remote compilation actually processes the change and produces a different binary.

## Background and Justification
The self-test needs to verify that:
1. The remote worker actually compiles the code (not returning cached results)
2. Code changes propagate correctly through rsync
3. The resulting binary reflects the change

A test code change provides a known modification that should produce a measurably different binary.

## Implementation Details

### Test Change Strategy
The change should be:
1. **Minimal**: Single file modification
2. **Detectable**: Produces a different binary hash
3. **Reversible**: Can be applied and reverted cleanly
4. **Deterministic**: Same change always produces same result

### Test Change Implementation
```rust
use std::fs;
use std::path::Path;

/// Represents a test modification to source code
pub struct TestCodeChange {
    pub file_path: PathBuf,
    pub original_content: String,
    pub modified_content: String,
    pub change_id: String,  // Unique identifier for this change
}

impl TestCodeChange {
    /// Create a test change for the main.rs file
    pub fn for_main_rs(project_dir: &Path) -> Result<Self> {
        let file_path = project_dir.join("src/main.rs");
        let original = fs::read_to_string(&file_path)?;

        // Generate a unique change ID based on timestamp
        let change_id = format\!("test_change_{}", chrono::Utc::now().timestamp());

        // Modify: add a const that will be compiled into the binary
        let modified = format\!(
            "{}\n\n// RCH Self-Test Change ID: {}\nconst RCH_TEST_MARKER: &str = \"{}\";\n",
            original, change_id, change_id
        );

        Ok(TestCodeChange {
            file_path,
            original_content: original,
            modified_content: modified,
            change_id,
        })
    }

    /// Apply the test change
    pub fn apply(&self) -> Result<()> {
        info\!("Applying test change {} to {:?}", self.change_id, self.file_path);
        fs::write(&self.file_path, &self.modified_content)?;
        Ok(())
    }

    /// Revert the test change
    pub fn revert(&self) -> Result<()> {
        info\!("Reverting test change {} from {:?}", self.change_id, self.file_path);
        fs::write(&self.file_path, &self.original_content)?;
        Ok(())
    }

    /// Check if binary contains the test marker
    pub fn verify_in_binary(&self, binary_path: &Path) -> Result<bool> {
        let binary_data = fs::read(binary_path)?;
        let marker_bytes = self.change_id.as_bytes();

        // Search for marker in binary
        for window in binary_data.windows(marker_bytes.len()) {
            if window == marker_bytes {
                return Ok(true);
            }
        }

        Ok(false)
    }
}

/// RAII guard for test changes - auto-reverts on drop
pub struct TestChangeGuard {
    change: TestCodeChange,
    applied: bool,
}

impl TestChangeGuard {
    pub fn new(change: TestCodeChange) -> Result<Self> {
        let mut guard = Self { change, applied: false };
        guard.change.apply()?;
        guard.applied = true;
        Ok(guard)
    }

    pub fn change_id(&self) -> &str {
        &self.change.change_id
    }

    pub fn verify_in_binary(&self, binary_path: &Path) -> Result<bool> {
        self.change.verify_in_binary(binary_path)
    }
}

impl Drop for TestChangeGuard {
    fn drop(&mut self) {
        if self.applied {
            if let Err(e) = self.change.revert() {
                error\!("Failed to revert test change: {}", e);
            }
        }
    }
}
