## Overview
Implement a utility for computing deterministic hashes of compiled binaries to verify that remote compilation produces correct outputs.

## Background and Justification
The self-test system needs to verify that remote workers actually compile code correctly. This requires:
1. Building the same code locally and remotely
2. Comparing the outputs to ensure they match (accounting for non-deterministic elements)
3. Detecting if a worker is misconfigured or producing corrupt binaries

## Implementation Details

### Binary Hash Strategy
Rust binaries contain non-deterministic elements:
- Build timestamps
- Absolute paths embedded in debug info
- Random identifiers in some metadata

We need a hash that ignores these while detecting real differences.

### Implementation
```rust
use sha2::{Sha256, Digest};
use std::fs::File;
use std::io::{BufReader, Read};
use object::{Object, ObjectSection};

pub struct BinaryHashResult {
    pub full_hash: String,           // Hash of entire file
    pub code_hash: String,           // Hash of code sections only
    pub text_section_size: u64,      // Size of .text section
    pub is_debug: bool,              // Contains debug info
}

/// Compute hash of the entire binary file
fn compute_full_hash(path: &Path) -> Result<String> {
    let file = File::open(path)?;
    let mut reader = BufReader::new(file);
    let mut hasher = Sha256::new();
    let mut buffer = [0u8; 65536];

    loop {
        let bytes_read = reader.read(&mut buffer)?;
        if bytes_read == 0 { break; }
        hasher.update(&buffer[..bytes_read]);
    }

    Ok(format\!("{:x}", hasher.finalize()))
}

/// Compute hash of code sections only (more deterministic)
fn compute_code_hash(path: &Path) -> Result<String> {
    let data = std::fs::read(path)?;
    let file = object::File::parse(&*data)?;

    let mut hasher = Sha256::new();

    // Hash only executable code sections
    for section in file.sections() {
        let name = section.name().unwrap_or("");
        if name == ".text" || name == ".rodata" || name.starts_with(".text.") {
            if let Ok(data) = section.data() {
                hasher.update(data);
            }
        }
    }

    Ok(format\!("{:x}", hasher.finalize()))
}

/// Extract metadata about the binary
fn extract_metadata(path: &Path) -> Result<(u64, bool)> {
    let data = std::fs::read(path)?;
    let file = object::File::parse(&*data)?;

    let text_size = file.sections()
        .filter(|s| s.name().unwrap_or("") == ".text")
        .map(|s| s.size())
        .sum();

    let has_debug = file.sections()
        .any(|s| s.name().unwrap_or("").starts_with(".debug"));

    Ok((text_size, has_debug))
}

/// Main hash computation function
pub fn compute_binary_hash(path: &Path) -> Result<BinaryHashResult> {
    let full_hash = compute_full_hash(path)?;
    let code_hash = compute_code_hash(path)?;
    let (text_section_size, is_debug) = extract_metadata(path)?;

    Ok(BinaryHashResult {
        full_hash,
        code_hash,
        text_section_size,
        is_debug,
    })
}

/// Compare two binaries for equivalence
pub fn binaries_equivalent(local: &BinaryHashResult, remote: &BinaryHashResult) -> bool {
    // Code hash must match exactly
    if local.code_hash \!= remote.code_hash {
        return false;
    }

    // Text section size should match
    if local.text_section_size \!= remote.text_section_size {
        return false;
    }

    // Debug status should match
    if local.is_debug \!= remote.is_debug {
        return false;
    }

    true
}
```

